2183	\paragraph{Measurement problem.}
2184	A quantum system $\mathbf{q}$ at fidelity \text{{\igfont 𐑐}} is coupled to a macroscopic
2185	apparatus $\mathbf{a}$ at \text{{\igfont 𐑱}}.  Because $\text{{\igprimfont ⋈}}$ is a bottleneck primitive under
2186	$\tensor$ (\Cref{def:bottleneck}):
2187	\begin{equation}
2188	  \text{{\igprimfont ⋈}}(\mathbf{q} \tensor \mathbf{a}) = \min(\text{{\igfont 𐑐}},\, \text{{\igfont 𐑱}}) = \text{{\igfont 𐑱}}.
2189	  \label{eq:f_collapse}
2190	\end{equation}
2191	Collapse is not a dynamical event appended to Schrödinger evolution; it is the
2192	algebraic consequence of $\text{{\igprimfont ⋈}}$ being a bottleneck.  No hidden variable, decoherence
2193	timescale, or many-worlds branch is required.  The ``when" of collapse is the
2194	instant of coupling; the ``which outcome" is determined by $\text{{\igprimfont ⊣}}$ --- the topology
2195	of the apparatus, which specifies the pointer observable.  The two questions that
2196	drive the measurement problem are answered by two different primitives.
